        {
            "id": "32239342",
            "user": "WikiHow Horizon",
            "timestamp": "13:10, 27 January 2024",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(14,551 bytes)",
            "change": 96,
            "summary": "(Updated link/s)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Cut a hole for the new outlet. Trace the outlet box on the wall where you want it to go. Then, use a keyhole saw (or strong utility knife) and make small and firm incisions over and over until you have cut through the wall material. Walls made with plaster may require you to drill holes into the corners and cut the hole with a Sawzall.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/electrical/21017174/how-to-add-an-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 8 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Install the outlet receptacle at the new end. Purchase the outlet and wires from a hardware or home improvement store and connect the wires to the receptacle. Inside the electrical cable, there are 3 conductor wires, typically black, white, and copper wire with no insulation. On the receptacle itself, there are 2 brass screws where the un-grounded or hot conductors attach, 2 silver screws for the grounded conductor or neutral conductors, and 1 green screw for the equipment ground conductor. <ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/electrical/21017174/how-to-add-an-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 11 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Install the cut-in outlet box with the fasteners supplied. The outlet box should be firm on the wall and not be able to be wiggled around. This is used to house the loose wires and keep them wrapped and secure for safety purposes. These are available at all home repair outlets.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/electrical/21017174/how-to-add-an-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 12 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Put everything in the wall and install wall plates. You'll need 2 machine-threaded screws to screw directly into the box. The outlet itself should then be screwed directly onto the box with the screws included.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/electrical/21017174/how-to-add-an-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 14 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the power on to test your work. The circuit should remain on and the fuse should not pop if you've installed the wires correctly. Test the outlet with a lamp, or some other low voltage device.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/electrical/21017174/how-to-add-an-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 15 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Get an electrician to inspect your work. Even if your region doesn’t require the work to be done by a certified electrician, you should still hire one to inspect the job. This could prevent issues down the line, including potential hazards like fire due to improper installation.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/electrical/21017174/how-to-add-an-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 17.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Cut a hole for the new outlet. Trace the outlet box on the wall where you want it to go. Then, use a keyhole saw (or strong utility knife) and make small and firm incisions over and over until you have cut through the wall material. Walls made with plaster may require you to drill holes into the corners and cut the hole with a Sawzall.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 8 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Install the outlet receptacle at the new end. Purchase the outlet and wires from a hardware or home improvement store and connect the wires to the receptacle. Inside the electrical cable, there are 3 conductor wires, typically black, white, and copper wire with no insulation. On the receptacle itself, there are 2 brass screws where the un-grounded or hot conductors attach, 2 silver screws for the grounded conductor or neutral conductors, and 1 green screw for the equipment ground conductor. <ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 11 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Install the cut-in outlet box with the fasteners supplied. The outlet box should be firm on the wall and not be able to be wiggled around. This is used to house the loose wires and keep them wrapped and secure for safety purposes. These are available at all home repair outlets.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 12 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Put everything in the wall and install wall plates. You'll need 2 machine-threaded screws to screw directly into the box. The outlet itself should then be screwed directly onto the box with the screws included.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 14 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the power on to test your work. The circuit should remain on and the fuse should not pop if you've installed the wires correctly. Test the outlet with a lamp, or some other low voltage device.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 15 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Get an electrician to inspect your work. Even if your region doesn’t require the work to be done by a certified electrician, you should still hire one to inspect the job. This could prevent issues down the line, including potential hazards like fire due to improper installation.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 17.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
