        {
            "id": "29138517",
            "user": "Sophia B",
            "timestamp": "20:26, 26 January 2021",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(14,489 bytes)",
            "change": -806,
            "summary": "(corrected a step)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Test the outlet with a multimeter. Set the multimeter to measure AC voltage. Put the red terminal into the shorter slot on the outlet, which should be hot, and the black terminal into the longer terminal, which should be negative.<ref>[v161396_b01]. 6 May 2020.</ref> If the outlet is working properly, the multimeter will show a reading between 110 and 120 volts.<ref>https://www.bhg.com/home-improvement/electrical/how-to-test-an-electrical-receptacle/</ref>"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Test the outlet and circuit with an amp meter. Most household circuits are 15 amps, and it's a good idea to test the circuit to make sure the new circuit won't overload it. Most electricians will do this after installation, to make sure the new circuit isn't stressing the source. To do this, turn on the circuit and everything connected to it so they’re drawing power. Put the black probe from an amp meter on the positive terminal screw and the white probe to the negative terminal screw to find the amps. Multiply the circuit voltage by the current to find the power in watts.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 16 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*If you already have the outlet cover in place, put the red terminal to the shorter slot on the outlet, which should be hot, and the black terminal into the longer terminal, which should be negative.<ref>[v161396_b01]. 6 May 2020.</ref>",
                    "#*Amp meters have a maximum current limit they’re able to test, so make sure your circuit doesn’t exceed that amount.",
                    "#*For most circuits, the maximum power available should be 1800 watts for everything on the circuit, but try to keep it around 1400 watts max to leave you a good safety margin."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "28734050",
            "user": "JustJellyKelly",
            "timestamp": "20:41, 12 October 2020",
