        {
            "id": "24625127",
            "user": "WikiHow Expert Review",
            "timestamp": "21:34, 21 May 2018",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(12,892 bytes)",
            "change": -577,
            "summary": "(Incorporating expert feedback)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Turn off the circuit breaker. It's absolutely essential that any time you're working with electricity you turn the current off and double-check to make sure there's no current before you start working. Always turn the circuit breaker off first, before doing anything else. [[Use Voltage Testers|Use a non-contact voltage tester]] to check before moving forward.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring-outlets/add-electrical-outlet/view-all/</ref>",
                    "#Assemble the necessary tools and materials. To install a new electrical outlet and make the connections safely, you'll need a few basic electricians' tools. Make sure you have access to the following:",
                    "#Determine the best location for the outlet. Measure the distance off the floor to the other outlets in the building. Be aware the bottom of the cover plate is not the distance you want to measure. Turn off power in the outlet. Take off the cover plate held on by the single screw in the middle of the outlet and measure to the hole cut in the wall.",
                    "#Find an opening in the wall. In most homes, there are {{convert|2x4|in|cm|abbr=on}} or {{convert|2x6|in|cm|abbr=on}} studs typically at {{convert|16 or 24|in|cm|abbr=on}} increments along any wall. Outlets must always be attached to a stud for security and safety. Mark a space at the appropriate height, at a location between 2 studs.",
                    "#Confirm there are no other mechanical or plumbing items in the wall. Check for plumbing from sinks showers or toilets on the opposite side of the wall. Be aware of any HVAC air returns or ducts.",
                    "#Find out where you can get power. There are 2 basic ways to install a new electrical outlet, running electrical wiring from a circuit breaker to the new location, or from another outlet in the same room. Depending on where you're installing a new outlet–living space, basement, or outdoor area–access to power may vary. Find the closest and most convenient place to connect to make the job easy on yourself.",
                    "#Pull the appropriate kind of wire between the 2 locations. Once you have access to the locations, get your Romex non-metallic wire. Give yourself {{convert|12 to 18|in|cm}} of wire sticking out on both ends. Wire the new receptacle first and then go back and connect the new wire to the existing circuit. Cut sheathing off Romex about {{convert|8|in|cm|1}} long.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring-outlets/add-electrical-outlet/view-all/</ref>",
                    "#*Your home may use 12- or 14-gauge wires. Check the circuit breaker to determine the correct amperage. For #14/2+g, use 15-amp wire and a 15-amp circuit breaker. Use 20-amp wire and a 20-amp circuit breaker for #12/2+g.",
                    "#Install the outlet receptacle at the new end. Purchase the outlet and wires from a hardware or home improvement store and connect the wires to the receptacle. Inside the electrical cable, there are 3 conductor wires, typically black, white, and copper wire with no insulation. On the receptacle itself, there are 2 brass screws where the un-grounded or hot conductors attach, 2 silver screws for the grounded conductor or neutral conductors, and 1 green screw for the equipment ground conductor. <ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>",
                    "#*Hook the bare copper wire around the ground wire scres and tighten the screw down.",
                    "#*Wrap the wire and connections with electrical tape to keep everything from touching.",
                    "#Install the cut-in outlet box with the fasteners supplied. The outlet box should be firm on the wall and not be able to be wiggled around. This is used to house the loose wires and keep them wrapped and secure for safety purposes. These are available at all home repair outlets.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>",
                    "#Install the wires at the starting outlet. Go back to starting outlet and connect the wires. Take the existing receptacle and remove it from the wires, then match up the black wires with black, white with white, and ground with ground.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring-outlets/add-electrical-outlet/view-all/</ref>",
                    "#*Cut an {{convert|8|in|cm|abbr=on}} pigtail (stubby piece of wire) and strip off both ends {{convert|3/4|in|cm}}. Take all 3 black wires together so the ends are matched up. The end of the new wire should meet the end of the old wire, and one end of the pigtail. The pigtail wire is the one that will be reconnected to the receptacle.",
                    "#Put everything in the wall and install wall plates. You'll only need 2 screws on the device, to screw directly into the box. The outlet itself should then be screwed directly onto the box with the screws included.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>",
                    "#Turn the power on to test your work. The circuit should remain on and the fuse should not pop if you've installed the wires correctly. Test the outlet with a lamp, or some other low voltage device.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>",
                    "#Test the outlet with an outlet circuit tester. Most household circuits are 15 amps, and it's a good idea to test the circuit to make sure the new circuit won't overload it. Most electricians will do this after installation, to make sure the new circuit isn't stressing the source. To do this, you'll need to use a voltage tester, connecting between the terminal screw on the breaker, and the other on the ground. Don't touch the neutral wire.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Turn off the circuit breaker. It's absolutely essential that any time you're working with electricity you turn the current off and double-check to make sure there's no current before you start working. Always turn the circuit breaker off first, before doing anything else. [[Use Voltage Testers|Use a non-contact voltage tester]] to check before moving forward.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring-outlets/add-electrical-outlet/view-all/</ref>[[Image:481328 1.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Assemble the necessary tools and materials. To install a new electrical outlet and make the connections safely, you'll need a few basic electricians' tools. Make sure you have access to the following: [[Image:481328 2.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Determine the best location for the outlet. Measure the distance off the floor to the other outlets in the building. Be aware the bottom of the cover plate is not the distance you want to measure. Turn off power in the outlet. Take off the cover plate held on by the single screw in the middle of the outlet and measure to the hole cut in the wall.[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find an opening in the wall. In most homes, there are {{convert|2x4|in|cm|abbr=on}} or {{convert|2x6|in|cm|abbr=on}} studs typically at {{convert|16 or 24|in|cm|abbr=on}} increments along any wall. Outlets must always be attached to a stud for security and safety. Mark a space at the appropriate height, at a location between 2 studs. [[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Confirm there are no other mechanical or plumbing items in the wall. Check for plumbing from sinks showers or toilets on the opposite side of the wall. Be aware of any HVAC air returns or ducts.[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find out where you can get power. There are 2 basic ways to install a new electrical outlet, running electrical wiring from a circuit breaker to the new location, or from another outlet in the same room. Depending on where you're installing a new outlet–living space, basement, or outdoor area–access to power may vary. Find the closest and most convenient place to connect to make the job easy on yourself.[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Pull the appropriate kind of wire between the 2 locations. Once you have access to the locations, get your Romex non-metallic wire. Give yourself {{convert|12 to 18|in|cm}} of wire sticking out on both ends. Wire the new receptacle first and then go back and connect the new wire to the existing circuit. Cut jacket off Romex about {{convert|8|in|cm|1}} long.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring-outlets/add-electrical-outlet/view-all/</ref>[[Image:481328 8.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#*Your home may use 12- or 14-gauge wires. Check the circuit breaker to determine the correct amperage. For #14/2+g, use 15 amp wire. Use 20 amp wire for #12/2+g.",
                    "#Install the outlet receptacle at the new end. Purchase the outlet and wires from a hardware or home improvement store and connect the wires to the receptacle. Inside the electrical cable, there are 3 conductor wires, typically black, white, and green with no insulation. On the receptacle itself, there are 2 brass screws where the un-grounded or hot conductors attach, 2 silver screws for the grounded conductor or neutral conductors, and 1 green screw for the equipment ground conductor. Wrap the wire and connections with electrical tape to keep everything from touching.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>",
                    "#*The green wire should be connected to the copper wire with no insulation. Hook it around and tighten green screw down.",
                    "#Install the cut-in outlet box with the fasteners supplied. The outlet box should be firm on the wall and not be able to be wiggled around. This is used to house the loose wires and keep them wrapped and secure for safety purposes. These are available at all home repair outlets.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 11 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Install the wires at the starting outlet. Go back to starting outlet and connect the wires. Take the existing receptacle and remove it from the wires, then match up the black wires with black, white with white, and ground with ground.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring-outlets/add-electrical-outlet/view-all/</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 13 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Cut an {{convert|8|in|cm|abbr=on}} pigtail (stubby piece of wire) and strip off both ends {{convert|3/4|in|cm}}. Take all 3 black wires together so the ends are matched up. The end of the new wire should meet the end of the old wire, and one end of pigtail.",
                    "#Put everything in the wall and install wall plates. You'll only need 2 screws on the device, to screw directly into the box. The outlet itself should then be screwed directly onto the box with the screws included.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:481328 12.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Turn the power on to test your work. The circuit should remain on and the fuse should not pop if you've installed the wires correctly. Test the outlet with a lamp, or some other low voltage device.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:481328 13.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Test the outlet with an outlet circuit tester. Most household circuits are 15 amps, and it's a good idea to test the circuit to make sure the new circuit won't overload it. Most electricians will do this after installation, to make sure the new circuit isn't stressing the source. To do this, you'll need to use a voltage tester, connecting between the terminal screw on the breaker, and the other on the ground. Don't touch the neutral wire.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 15 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "24595388",
            "user": "WikiHow Expert Review",
            "timestamp": "18:41, 16 May 2018",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(13,469 bytes)",
            "change": -330,
            "summary": "(Incorporating expert feedback)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Distributing power outlets evenly throughout a space can open up rooms and make new spaces much more friendly. Placing a new electrical outlet can help make a house more livable. With the right planning and safety precautions, running wire from a power source to a new outlet doesn't need to take more than a couple of hours. Learn to plan the job properly, run wire safely, and test your project to make sure it's secure.",
                    "#Cut a hole for the new outlet. Trace the outlet box on the wall where you want it to go. Then, use a keyhole saw (or strong utility knife) and make small and firm incisions over and over until you have cut through the wall material. Walls made with plaster may require you to drill holes into the corners and cut the hole with a hole saw.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>",
                    "#Run the wire from the power source to the new outlet. It’s best to avoid making additional holes in the wall if at all possible. Look for ways to extend the branch circuit wiring vertically from the source of power, such as through an attic or basement. Or, use an external method such as conduit or wire mold, or run a fish tape through the hole for the new outlet to run the conductors.",
                    "#*If you can’t find a way to run the wire, you may have to cut a hole in the wall near the existing power source.",
                    "#Pull the appropriate kind of wire between the 2 locations. Once you have access to the locations, get your Romex non-metallic wire. Give yourself {{convert|12 to 18|in|cm}} of wire sticking out on both ends. Wire the new receptacle first and then go back and connect the new wire to the existing circuit. Cut jacket off Romex about {{convert|8|in|cm|1}} long.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring-outlets/add-electrical-outlet/view-all/</ref>[[Image:481328 8.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#*Your home may use 12- or 14-gauge wires. Check the circuit breaker to determine the correct amperage. For #14/2+g, use 15 amp wire. Use 20 amp wire for #12/2+g.",
                    "#*You should use wire that is proper for the power rating of that circuit and of the same size as the wire you are connecting to. Take a sample of the existing wire with you when you go to purchase the new wire.",
                    "#Install the outlet receptacle at the new end. Purchase the outlet and wires from a hardware or home improvement store and connect the wires to the receptacle. Inside the electrical cable, there are 3 conductor wires, typically black, white, and green with no insulation. On the receptacle itself, there are 2 brass screws where the un-grounded or hot conductors attach, 2 silver screws for the grounded conductor or neutral conductors, and 1 green screw for the equipment ground conductor. Wrap the wire and connections with electrical tape to keep everything from touching.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "Spreading power outlets evenly throughout a space can open up rooms and make new spaces much more friendly. Placing a new electrical outlet can help make a house more livable. With the right planning and safety precautions, running wire from a power source to a new outlet doesn't need to take more than a couple of hours. Learn to plan the job properly, run wire safely, and test your project to make sure it's secure.",
                    "#Cut a hole in the wall near the existing power source. To open up enough space to work, you'll need to cut a {{convert|12x12|in|cm|abbr=on}} piece of drywall out of the wall near the receptacle, preferably above it. You'll also need to cut a new hole, roughly the same height, at the location where you want the new outlet. This can be somewhat smaller: {{convert|2.5x2|in|cm|abbr=on}} to account for the new cut-in box.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*To make your cuts, use a keyhole saw (or strong utility knife) and make small and firm incisions over and over until you have cut through the wall material. Walls made with plaster may require you to drill holes into the corners and cut the hole with a hole saw.",
                    "#*You also might have to cut one other hole in between 2 locations depending on how far they are apart. In general, you want to have access holes every {{convert|3|ft|m|1}} so it’s easier to thread the wires through the wall manually.",
                    "#*If you don’t want to cut a hole in your drywall, use an external method such as conduit or wire mold. Or, you can run a fish tape through the hole for the new outlet to run the conductors.",
                    "#Pull the appropriate kind of wire between the 2 locations. Once you have access to the locations, get your Romex non-metallic wire. For residential jobs, the appropriate size of wire is 14/2, rated for 15 amps. Give yourself {{convert|12 to 18|in|cm}} of wire sticking out on both ends. Wire the new receptacle first and then go back and connect the new wire to the existing circuit. Cut jacket off Romex about {{convert|8|in|cm|1}} long.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring-outlets/add-electrical-outlet/view-all/</ref>[[Image:481328 8.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#*You should use wire that is proper for the power rating of that circuit and of the same size as the wire you are connecting too. Take a sample of the existing wire with you when you go to purchase the new wire.",
                    "#Install the outlet receptacle at the new end. Purchase the outlet and wires from a hardware or home improvement store and connect the wires to the receptacle. Inside the electrical cable, there are 3 conductor wires, typically black, white, and green with no insulation. On the receptacle itself, there are 2 brass screws where the un-grounded or hot conductors attach, 2 silver screws for the grounded conductor or neutral conductors, and 1 green screw for the equipment ground conductor. Wrap the wire and connections with electrical tape to keep everything from touching.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 12 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "",
                    ""
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "24555369",
            "user": "Votebot",
