        {
            "id": "24477850",
            "user": "WikiHow Expert Review",
            "timestamp": "18:32, 30 April 2018",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(13,797 bytes)",
            "change": 2038,
            "summary": "(Incorporating expert feedback)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Find out if you need a permit or professional installation. Depending on where you live, you may need a permit to add an electrical outlet in your home. In some places, you may need to hire a professional to do any electrical work. Check with your local code office to find out if either of these pertains to your area.",
                    "#Turn off the circuit breaker. It's absolutely essential that any time you're working with electricity you turn the current off and double-check to make sure there's no current before you start working. Always turn the circuit breaker off first, before doing anything else. [[Use Voltage Testers|Use a non-contact voltage tester]] to check before moving forward.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring-outlets/add-electrical-outlet/view-all/</ref>[[Image:481328 1.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#*Keyhole saw",
                    "#*Lineman’s pliers",
                    "#*Needle-nose pliers",
                    "#*A fish tape",
                    "#Find an opening in the wall. In most homes, there are {{convert|2x4|in|cm|abbr=on}} or {{convert|2x6|in|cm|abbr=on}} studs typically at {{convert|16 or 24|in|cm|abbr=on}} increments along any wall. Outlets must always be attached to a stud for security and safety. Mark a space at the appropriate height, at a location between 2 studs. [[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find out where you can get power. There are 2 basic ways to install a new electrical outlet, running electrical wiring from a circuit breaker to the new location, or from another outlet in the same room. Depending on where you're installing a new outlet–living space, basement, or outdoor area–access to power may vary. Find the closest and most convenient place to connect to make the job easy on yourself.[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Be sure to consider the existing electrical load and what the new load will add to the existing branch circuit. Calculate the load to ensure it is safe. The most convenient source of power may not be the safest!",
                    "#Cut a hole in the wall near the existing power source. To open up enough space to work, you'll need to cut a {{convert|12x12|in|cm|abbr=on}} piece of drywall out of the wall near the receptacle, preferably above it. You'll also need to cut a new hole, roughly the same height, at the location where you want the new outlet. This can be somewhat smaller: {{convert|2.5x2|in|cm|abbr=on}} to account for the new cut-in box.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*To make your cuts, use a keyhole saw (or strong utility knife) and make small and firm incisions over and over until you have cut through the wall material. Walls made with plaster may require you to drill holes into the corners and cut the hole with a hole saw.",
                    "#*You also might have to cut one other hole in between 2 locations depending on how far they are apart. In general, you want to have access holes every {{convert|3|ft|m|1}} so it’s easier to thread the wires through the wall manually.",
                    "#*If you don’t want to cut a hole in your drywall, use an external method such as conduit or wire mold. Or, you can run a fish tape through the hole for the new outlet to run the conductors.",
                    "#Pull the appropriate kind of wire between the 2 locations. Once you have access to the locations, get your Romex non-metallic wire. For residential jobs, the appropriate size of wire is 14/2, rated for 15 amps. Give yourself {{convert|12 to 18|in|cm}} of wire sticking out on both ends. Wire the new receptacle first and then go back and connect the new wire to the existing circuit. Cut jacket off Romex about {{convert|8|in|cm|1}} long.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring-outlets/add-electrical-outlet/view-all/</ref>[[Image:481328 8.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Install the outlet receptacle at the new end. Purchase the outlet and wires from a hardware or home improvement store and connect the wires to the receptacle. Inside the electrical cable, there are 3 conductor wires, typically black, white, and green with no insulation. On the receptacle itself, there are 2 brass screws where the un-grounded or hot conductors attach, 2 silver screws for the grounded conductor or neutral conductors, and 1 green screw for the equipment ground conductor. Wrap the wire and connections with electrical tape to keep everything from touching.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 12 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Strip the insulation {{convert|3/4|in|cm}} off the end of the white wire. Bend a hook on the end of the wire using pliers, then put the hook around 1 of silver screws and tighten the screw in a clockwise direction. The other silver screw isn’t used.",
                    "#Install the cut-in outlet box with the fasteners supplied. The outlet box should be firm on the wall and not be able to be wiggled around. This is used to house the loose wires and keep them wrapped and secure for safety purposes. These are available at all home repair outlets.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 11 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Install the wires at the starting outlet. Go back to starting outlet and connect the wires. Take the existing receptacle and remove it from the wires, then match up the black wires with black, white with white, and ground with ground.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring-outlets/add-electrical-outlet/view-all/</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 13 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Cut an {{convert|8|in|cm|abbr=on}} pigtail (stubby piece of wire) and strip off both ends {{convert|3/4|in|cm}}. Take all 3 black wires together so the ends are matched up. The end of the new wire should meet the end of the old wire, and one end of pigtail.",
                    "#*Get a wire nut (plastic cone) that just screws onto the wire to connect them. Use lineman’s pliers to twist the 3 black wires together, then twist on an appropriately-sized wire nut. The same goes for white and ground wires, respectively. Take the other end of the pigtail and attach it to the screws the same way as before: green goes to green, white goes silver, black goes to bronze.",
                    "#Put everything in the wall and install wall plates. You'll only need 2 screws on the device, to screw directly into the box. The outlet itself should then be screwed directly onto the box with the screws included.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:481328 12.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Turn the power on to test your work. The circuit should remain on and the fuse should not pop if you've installed the wires correctly. Test the outlet with a lamp, or some other low voltage device.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:481328 13.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Test the outlet with an outlet circuit tester. Most household circuits are 15 amps, and it's a good idea to test the circuit to make sure the new circuit won't overload it. Most electricians will do this after installation, to make sure the new circuit isn't stressing the source. To do this, you'll need to use a voltage tester, connecting between the terminal screw on the breaker, and the other on the ground. Don't touch the neutral wire.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 15 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*For most circuits, the maximum power available should be 1800 watts for everything on the circuit, but try to keep it around 1400 watts max to leave you a good safety margin.",
                    "#Get an electrician to inspect your work. Even if your region doesn’t require the work to be done by a certified electrician, you should still hire one to inspect the job. This could prevent issues down the line, including potential hazards like a fire due to improper installation.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/how-to/how-to-add-electrical-outlet</ref>",
                    "==Sources and Citations==",
                    "{{reflist}}",
                    "__PARTS__"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "",
                    "",
                    "#Turn off the circuit breaker. It's absolutely essential that any time you're working with electricity you turn the current off and double-check to make sure there's no current before you start working. Always turn the circuit breaker off first, before doing anything else. [[Use Voltage Testers|Use a non-contact voltage tester]] to check before moving forward. [[Image:481328 1.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#*Pair of lineman’s pliers",
                    "#Find an opening in the wall. In most homes, there are 2x4 or 2x6 studs typically at 16\" or 24\" increments along any wall. Outlets must always be attached to a stud for security and safety. Mark a space at the appropriate height, at a location between two studs. [[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find out where you can get power. There are two basic ways to install a new electrical outlet, running electrical wiring from a circuit breaker to the new location, or from another outlet in the same room. Depending on where you're installing a new outlet–living space, basement, or outdoor area–access to power may vary. Find the closest and most convenient place to connect to make the job easy on yourself.[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Cut a hole in the wall near the existing power source. To open up enough space to work, you'll need to cut a 12x12 piece of drywall out of the wall near the receptacle, preferably above it. You'll also need to cut a new hole, roughly the same height, at the location where you want the new outlet. This can be somewhat smaller: 2.5-in. x {{convert|2|in|cm|1}} to account for the new cut-in box. [[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*To make your cuts, use a strong utility knife and make small and firm incisions over and over until you have cut through the wall material. Walls made with plaster may require you to drill holes into the corners and cut the hole with a hole saw.",
                    "#*You also might have to cut one other hole in between two locations depending on how far they are apart. In general, you want to have access holes every {{convert|3|ft|m|1}} so it’s easier to thread the wires through the wall manually.",
                    "#Pull the appropriate kind of wire between the two locations. Once you have access to the locations, get your Romex non-metallic wire. For residential jobs, the appropriate size of wire is 14/2, rated for 15 amps. Give yourself a foot to {{convert|18|in|cm|1}} of wire sticking out on both ends. Wire the new receptacle first and then go back and connect the new wire to the existing circuit. Cut jacket off Romex about {{convert|8|in|cm|1}} long. [[Image:481328 8.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Install the outlet receptacle at the new end. After you run the wire between the two locations, make your connections and wrap the wire. Purchase the outlet from a store and connect the wires to receptacle. Inside the wire, there are three conductors, typically black, white, and green with no insulation. On the receptacle itself, there are two brass screws, two silver screws, and green screw. It's typically good practice to wrap the wire and connections with electrical tape, just to keep everything from touching. Screws and wire should all be wrapped.[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 12 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*The white wire needs to be stripped ¾-inch off end of wire. Bend a hook on the end of the wire, put hook around one of silver screws and then tighten screw down. The other silver screw isn’t used.",
                    "#Install the cut-in outlet box with the fasteners supplied. The outlet box should be firm on the wall and not be able to be wiggled around. This is used to house the loose wires and keep them wrapped and secure for safety purposes. These are available at all home repair outlets.[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 11 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Install the wires at the starting outlet. Go back to starting outlet and connect the wires. Take the existing receptacle and remove it from the wires, then match up the black wires with black, white with white, and ground with ground.[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 13 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Cut an 8-inch pig-tail (stubby piece of wire) and strip off both ends 3/4s of an inch. Take all three black wires together so the ends are matched up. The end of the new wire should meet the end of the old wire, and one end of pigtail.",
                    "#*Get a wire nut (plastic cone) that just screws onto the wire to connect them. The three black wires should be able to connect with one wire nut, and the same goes for white and ground wires. Take the other end of the pigtail and attach it to the screws the same way as before: green goes to green, white goes silver, black goes to bronze.",
                    "#Put everything in the wall and install wall plates. You'll only need two screws on the device, to screw directly into the box. The outlet itself should then be screwed directly onto the box with the screws included. [[Image:481328 12.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Turn the power on to test your work. The circuit should remain on and the fuse should not pop if you've installed the wires correctly. Test the outlet with a lamp, or some other low voltage device. [[Image:481328 13.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Test the outlet with an outlet circuit tester. Most household circuits are 15 amps, and it's a good idea to test the circuit to make sure the new circuit won't overload it. Most electricians will do this after installation, to make sure the new circuit isn't stressing the source. To do this, you'll need to use a voltage tester, connecting between the terminal screw on the breaker, and the other on the ground. Don't touch the neutral wire.[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 15 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*For most circuits, the maximum power available should be 1800 watts for everything on this circuit, but it's usually common to reserve about 20% in case you need to plug a high-watt appliance into the outlet. Try to keep it around 1400 watts max, to leave you a good safety margin.",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "__Part__"
                ]
            }
        },
