        {
            "id": "19886512",
            "user": "Wikiphoto",
            "timestamp": "19:19, 25 March 2016",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(11,768 bytes)",
            "change": -3373,
            "summary": "(Reverted edits by Wikivisual (Talk); changed back to last version by Votebot)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "revert",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Turn off the circuit breaker. It's absolutely essential that any time you're working with electricity you turn the current off and double-check to make sure there's no current before you start working. Always turn the circuit breaker off first, before doing anything else. [[Use Voltage Testers|Use a non-contact voltage tester]] to check before moving forward. [[Image:481328 1.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Assemble the necessary tools and materials. To install a new electrical outlet and make the connections safely, you'll need a few basic electricians' tools. Make sure you have access to the following: [[Image:481328 2.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Determine the best location for the outlet. Measure the distance off the floor to the other outlets in the building. Be aware the bottom of the cover plate is not the distance you want to measure. Turn off power in the outlet. Take off the cover plate held on by the single screw in the middle of the outlet and measure to the hole cut in the wall.[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find an opening in the wall. In most homes, there are 2x4 or 2x6 studs typically at 16\" or 24\" increments along any wall. Outlets must always be attached to a stud for security and safety. Mark a space at the appropriate height, at a location between two studs. [[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Confirm there are no other mechanical or plumbing items in the wall. Check for plumbing from sinks showers or toilets on the opposite side of the wall. Be aware of any HVAC air returns or ducts.[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find out where you can get power. There are two basic ways to install a new electrical outlet, running electrical wiring from a circuit breaker to the new location, or from another outlet in the same room. Depending on where you're installing a new outlet–living space, basement, or outdoor area–access to power may vary. Find the closest and most convenient place to connect to make the job easy on yourself.[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Cut a hole in the wall near the existing power source. To open up enough space to work, you'll need to cut a 12x12 piece of drywall out of the wall near the receptacle, preferably above it. You'll also need to cut a new hole, roughly the same height, at the location where you want the new outlet. This can be somewhat smaller: 2.5-in. x {{convert|2|in|cm|1}} to account for the new cut-in box. [[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Pull the appropriate kind of wire between the two locations. Once you have access to the locations, get your Romex non-metallic wire. For residential jobs, the appropriate size of wire is 14/2, rated for 15 amps. Give yourself a foot to {{convert|18|in|cm|1}} of wire sticking out on both ends. Wire the new receptacle first and then go back and connect the new wire to the existing circuit. Cut jacket off Romex about {{convert|8|in|cm|1}} long. [[Image:481328 8.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Install the outlet receptacle at the new end. After you run the wire between the two locations, make your connections and wrap the wire. Purchase the outlet from a store and connect the wires to receptacle. Inside the wire, there are three conductors, typically black, white, and green with no insulation. On the receptacle itself, there are two brass screws, two silver screws, and green screw. It's typically good practice to wrap the wire and connections with electrical tape, just to keep everything from touching. Screws and wire should all be wrapped.[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 12 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Install the cut-in outlet box with the fasteners supplied. The outlet box should be firm on the wall and not be able to be wiggled around. This is used to house the loose wires and keep them wrapped and secure for safety purposes. These are available at all home repair outlets.[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 11 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Install the wires at the starting outlet. Go back to starting outlet and connect the wires. Take the existing receptacle and remove it from the wires, then match up the black wires with black, white with white, and ground with ground.[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 13 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Put everything in the wall and install wall plates. You'll only need two screws on the device, to screw directly into the box. The outlet itself should then be screwed directly onto the box with the screws included. [[Image:481328 12.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Turn the power on to test your work. The circuit should remain on and the fuse should not pop if you've installed the wires correctly. Test the outlet with a lamp, or some other low voltage device. [[Image:481328 13.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Test the outlet with an outlet circuit tester. Most household circuits are 15 amps, and it's a good idea to test the circuit to make sure the new circuit won't overload it. Most electricians will do this after installation, to make sure the new circuit isn't stressing the source. To do this, you'll need to use a voltage tester, connecting between the terminal screw on the breaker, and the other on the ground. Don't touch the neutral wire.[[Image:Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 15 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Turn off the circuit breaker. It's absolutely essential that any time you're working with electricity you turn the current off and double-check to make sure there's no current before you start working. Always turn the circuit breaker off first, before doing anything else. [[Use Voltage Testers|Use a non-contact voltage tester]] to check before moving forward.<br><br>{{whvid|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 1.360p.mp4|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 1-preview.jpg|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 1 Version 3.jpg|gif=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 1.360p.gif|giffirst=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 1.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Assemble the necessary tools and materials. To install a new electrical outlet and make the connections safely, you'll need a few basic electricians' tools. Make sure you have access to the following:<br><br>{{whvid|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 2.360p.mp4|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 2-preview.jpg|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 2 Version 3.jpg|gif=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Determine the best location for the outlet. Measure the distance off the floor to the other outlets in the building. Be aware the bottom of the cover plate is not the distance you want to measure. Turn off power in the outlet. Take off the cover plate held on by the single screw in the middle of the outlet and measure to the hole cut in the wall.<br><br>{{whvid|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 3.360p.mp4|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 3-preview.jpg|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 3 Version 3.jpg|gif=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 3.360p.gif|giffirst=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 3.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Find an opening in the wall. In most homes, there are 2x4 or 2x6 studs typically at 16\" or 24\" increments along any wall. Outlets must always be attached to a stud for security and safety. Mark a space at the appropriate height, at a location between two studs.<br><br>{{whvid|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 4.360p.mp4|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 4-preview.jpg|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 4 Version 3.jpg|gif=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Confirm there are no other mechanical or plumbing items in the wall. Check for plumbing from sinks showers or toilets on the opposite side of the wall. Be aware of any HVAC air returns or ducts.<br><br>{{whvid|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 5.360p.mp4|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 5-preview.jpg|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 5 Version 3.jpg|gif=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 5.360p.gif|giffirst=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 5.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Find out where you can get power. There are two basic ways to install a new electrical outlet, running electrical wiring from a circuit breaker to the new location, or from another outlet in the same room. Depending on where you're installing a new outlet–living space, basement, or outdoor area–access to power may vary. Find the closest and most convenient place to connect to make the job easy on yourself.<br><br>{{whvid|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 6.360p.mp4|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 6-preview.jpg|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 6 Version 3.jpg|gif=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 6.360p.gif|giffirst=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 6.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Cut a hole in the wall near the existing power source. To open up enough space to work, you'll need to cut a 12x12 piece of drywall out of the wall near the receptacle, preferably above it. You'll also need to cut a new hole, roughly the same height, at the location where you want the new outlet. This can be somewhat smaller: 2.5-in. x {{convert|2|in|cm|1}} to account for the new cut-in box.<br><br>{{whvid|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 7.360p.mp4|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 7-preview.jpg|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 7 Version 3.jpg|gif=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 7.360p.gif|giffirst=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 7.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Pull the appropriate kind of wire between the two locations. Once you have access to the locations, get your Romex non-metallic wire. For residential jobs, the appropriate size of wire is 14/2, rated for 15 amps. Give yourself a foot to {{convert|18|in|cm|1}} of wire sticking out on both ends. Wire the new receptacle first and then go back and connect the new wire to the existing circuit. Cut jacket off Romex about {{convert|8|in|cm|1}} long.<br><br>{{whvid|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 8.360p.mp4|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 8-preview.jpg|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 8 Version 3.jpg|gif=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 8.360p.gif|giffirst=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 8.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Install the outlet receptacle at the new end. After you run the wire between the two locations, make your connections and wrap the wire. Purchase the outlet from a store and connect the wires to receptacle. Inside the wire, there are three conductors, typically black, white, and green with no insulation. On the receptacle itself, there are two brass screws, two silver screws, and green screw. It's typically good practice to wrap the wire and connections with electrical tape, just to keep everything from touching. Screws and wire should all be wrapped.<br><br>{{whvid|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 9.360p.mp4|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 9-preview.jpg|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 9 Version 3.jpg|gif=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 9.360p.gif|giffirst=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 9.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Install the cut-in outlet box with the fasteners supplied. The outlet box should be firm on the wall and not be able to be wiggled around. This is used to house the loose wires and keep them wrapped and secure for safety purposes. These are available at all home repair outlets.<br><br>{{whvid|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 10.360p.mp4|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 10-preview.jpg|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 10 Version 3.jpg|gif=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 10.360p.gif|giffirst=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 10.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Install the wires at the starting outlet. Go back to starting outlet and connect the wires. Take the existing receptacle and remove it from the wires, then match up the black wires with black, white with white, and ground with ground.<br><br>{{whvid|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 11.360p.mp4|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 11-preview.jpg|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 11 Version 3.jpg|gif=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 11.360p.gif|giffirst=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 11.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Put everything in the wall and install wall plates. You'll only need two screws on the device, to screw directly into the box. The outlet itself should then be screwed directly onto the box with the screws included.<br><br>{{whvid|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 12.360p.mp4|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 12-preview.jpg|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 12 Version 3.jpg|gif=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 12.360p.gif|giffirst=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 12.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Turn the power on to test your work. The circuit should remain on and the fuse should not pop if you've installed the wires correctly. Test the outlet with a lamp, or some other low voltage device.<br><br>{{whvid|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 13.360p.mp4|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 13-preview.jpg|Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 13 Version 3.jpg|gif=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 13.360p.gif|giffirst=Add an Electrical Outlet to a Wall Step 13.360p.first.gif}}",
