        {
            "id": "16219502",
            "user": "Wikiphoto",
            "timestamp": "06:15, 24 December 2014",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(12,292 bytes)",
            "change": 178,
            "summary": "(adding images)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Turn off the circuit breaker. It's absolutely essential that any time you're working with electricity you turn the current off and double-check to make sure there's no current before you start working. Always turn the circuit breaker off first, before doing anything else. [[Use Voltage Testers|Use a non-contact voltage tester]] to check before moving forward. [[Image:481328 1.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Assemble the necessary tools and materials. To install a new electrical outlet and make the connections safely, you'll need a few basic electricians' tools. Make sure you have access to the following: [[Image:481328 2.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Pull the appropriate kind of wire between the two locations. Once you have access to the locations, get your Romex non-metallic wire. For residential jobs, the appropriate size of wire is 14/2, rated for 15 amps. Give yourself a foot to {{convert|18|in|cm|1}} of wire sticking out on both ends. Wire the new receptacle first and then go back and connect the new wire to the existing circuit. Cut jacket off Romex about {{convert|8|in|cm|1}} long. [[Image:481328 8.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Put everything in the wall and install wall plates. You'll only need two screws on the device, to screw directly into the box. The outlet itself should then be screwed directly onto the box with the screws included. [[Image:481328 12.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Turn the power on to test your work. The circuit should remain on and the fuse should not pop if you've installed the wires correctly. Test the outlet with a lamp, or some other low voltage device. [[Image:481328 13.jpg|center|416px]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Turn off the circuit breaker. It's absolutely essential that any time you're working with electricity you turn the current off and double-check to make sure there's no current before you start working. Always turn the circuit breaker off first, before doing anything else. [[Use Voltage Testers|Use a non-contact voltage tester]] to check before moving forward.",
                    "#Assemble the necessary tools and materials. To install a new electrical outlet and make the connections safely, you'll need a few basic electricians' tools. Make sure you have access to the following:",
                    "#Pull the appropriate kind of wire between the two locations. Once you have access to the locations, get your Romex non-metallic wire. For residential jobs, the appropriate size of wire is 14/2, rated for 15 amps. Give yourself a foot to {{convert|18|in|cm|1}} of wire sticking out on both ends. Wire the new receptacle first and then go back and connect the new wire to the existing circuit. Cut jacket off Romex about {{convert|8|in|cm|1}} long.",
                    "#Put everything in the wall and install wall plates. You'll only need two screws on the device, to screw directly into the box. The outlet itself should then be screwed directly onto the box with the screws included.",
                    "#Turn the power on to test your work. The circuit should remain on and the fuse should not pop if you've installed the wires correctly. Test the outlet with a lamp, or some other low voltage device."
                ]
            }
        },
